I&#39;ve got another one, that is somewhat more situational. It doesn&#39;t always apply.&nbsp;<strong>Apply the rule of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yes,_and..." rel="nofollow">&#39;Yes, And...&#39;</a> from Improv Comedy</strong>&nbsp;this one is far more situational. As a GM you have to be able to say no at points. However, if there is no reason to say no (ie, plot, challenge, complete ridiculousness - which in itself is not always a reason to say no) then follow the rule of saying Yes, and.... (adding to the scene) instead of saying no. No turns off possiblities. No ends discussions. No changes things from progressing to stopping. Its not a very beginner rule, more an Intermediate one but very often it is better to continue the thread and let it move on to see what happens. More often than not this makes for great sessions.

[/quote]

This is a good one.&nbsp; Within the realm of gaming, I&#39;ve heard it as <b>Either Say Yes or Roll the Dice</b>.

Is there a fire extinguisher in that burning building?&nbsp; Why not?&nbsp; What does it hurt the story for there not to be?&nbsp;&nbsp;

Is there a high press fire hose?&nbsp; Well, maybe. Roll some Perception to find out.&nbsp;&nbsp;
